    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The 6th century BCE witnessed the rise of 16 major territorial states known as Mahajanapadas, marking the Second Urbanization in ancient India.",
        "Mahajanapadas were broadly classified into two political systems: Monarchies (Rajyas) and Republics or Oligarchies (Gana-Sanghas).",
        "Magadha emerged as the supreme imperial power due to its strategic location, fertile agricultural plains, rich iron ore deposits, and powerful rulers like Bimbisara and Ajatashatru.",
        "Vajji and Malla were prominent Gana-Sanghas where political power was shared among an assembly of clan elders.",
        "The widespread adoption of iron technology and punch-marked coins significantly boosted trade, agriculture, and state revenues."
      ],
      "id": "9knwuny"
    },
